<div id=3D"outline-container-3" class=3D"outline-2">
<h2 id=3D"3">sdl3 bindings</h2>
<div class=3D"outline-text-2" id=3D"text-3">
<p>
Archive: <a href=3D"https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/ann-sdl3-bindings/18415/1">=
https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/ann-sdl3-bindings/18415/1</a>
</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"outline-container-orgbc74cd5" class=3D"outline-3">
<h3 id=3D"orgbc74cd5">sanette announced</h3>
<div class=3D"outline-text-3" id=3D"text-orgbc74cd5">
<p>
Hello!
</p>

<p>
it's my pleasure to announce a (very) preliminary version of <code>OCaml-sd=
l3</code> (or simply <code>sdl3</code> for the package name), which obvious=
ly is a set of bindings for the <a href=3D"https://www.libsdl.org/">SDL3</a=
> library.
</p>

<p>
<a href=3D"https://github.com/sanette/ocaml-sdl3">https://github.com/sanett=
e/ocaml-sdl3</a>
</p>

<p>
Why announcing a preliminary version? First, because I won't have much time=
 to work on it in the next couple of months, and second, because I believe =
you can already have fun with it!
</p>

<p>
The bindings are generated by a program that uses <code>clang</code> to ins=
pect the SDL code. This is a first phase; not all functions are usable yet.=
  I have started to write manual wrappers for a few of them. (See the examp=
les).
</p>

<p>
<span class=3D"underline">AI disclaimer:</span> Although I have to admit ch=
atGPT was my friend for understanding how to use <code>clang</code> and <co=
de>ctypes</code>, I would like to state here that the whole binding generat=
or (plus all manual additions, of course) was entirely written by (my) hand.
</p>
</div>
