<div id=3D"outline-container-org75a2330" class=3D"outline-3">
<h3 id=3D"org75a2330">C=C3=A9dric announced</h3>
<div class=3D"outline-text-3" id=3D"text-org75a2330">
<p>
Hello everyone,
</p>

<p>
<a href=3D"https://github.com/cedricbonhomme/iterated-function-systems">ifs=
-fractals</a> =E2=80=94 a small library and CLI that draws the attractors o=
f iterated function systems (the Barnsley fern and friends) with the chaos =
game =E2=80=94 just got its first feature release since arriving on opam th=
is summer. After sixteen years of intensive development ;-)
</p>


<div id=3D"orgfff49ac" class=3D"figure">
<p><img src=3D"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex020/uploads/ocaml/original=
/2X/6/69533442f671ef2ba37fb0c5029780b8ec8b10c5.png" alt=3D"69533442f671ef2b=
a37fb0c5029780b8ec8b10c5.png" width=3D"80%" />
</p>
</div>

<p>
What's new in 1.1.0:
</p>

<ul class=3D"org-ul">
<li><p>
Headless PNG rendering. Until now the fractals could only be drawn into a g=
raphics window. You can now render straight to a file, no display needed:
</p>
<pre class=3D"example" id=3D"org23f9415">
ifs-fractals -o fern.png -s 800x1280 barnsley
</pre></li>
<li>or from code, save_png barnsley 200000 "fern.png". This makes the packa=
ge usable on servers and in CI, and made testing much easier. A fun constra=
int I set myself: no new dependencies =E2=80=94 the PNG writer is self-cont=
ained, writing 8-bit PNGs with stored deflate blocks in pure OCaml.</li>
<li><p>
Density coloring. The chaos game doesn't visit the attractor uniformly =E2=
=80=94 some regions are hit far more often than others, and that density is=
 where much of the visual structure lives. With -c (or save_png \~color:tru=
e), pixels are colored by  their hit count on a log scale. The image above =
is the result: the fern gets its veins back.
</p>
<pre class=3D"example" id=3D"orge096504">
ifs-fractals -c -o fern.png -s 800x1280 barnsley
</pre></li>
</ul>

<p>
Install or upgrade with opam install ifs-fractals, then try ifs-fractals &n=
dash;list to see all sixteen attractors.
</p>

<p>
If you're curious how new attractors get designed =E2=80=94 how one finds t=
he six coefficients per affine map that produce a maple leaf or a sunflower=
 =E2=80=94 I wrote the techniques up in a short paper, Building New IFS Att=
ractors: a Working Vocabulary of Affine Maps ( <a href=3D"https://doi.org/1=
0.5281/zenodo.21804778">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21804778</a> ), with=
 a more narrative account on my blog ( <a href=3D"https://www.cedricbonhomm=
e.org/2026/07/27/challenging-claude-with-ifs-fractals/">https://www.cedricb=
onhomme.org/2026/07/27/challenging-claude-with-ifs-fractals/</a> ).
</p>

<p>
One disclosure, since it's the interesting part of the story: the new attra=
ctors were designed by Claude (Anthropic's Fable 5 model) =E2=80=94 the blo=
g post recounts that experiment. Everything else =E2=80=94 the library, the=
 CLI, the PNG writer, and the paper itself =E2=80=94 is my own work, not LL=
M output.
</p>

<ul class=3D"org-ul">
<li>Repository: <a href=3D"https://github.com/cedricbonhomme/iterated-funct=
ion-systems">https://github.com/cedricbonhomme/iterated-function-systems</a=
></li>
<li>opam: <a href=3D"https://opam.ocaml.org/packages/ifs-fractals/">https:/=
/opam.ocaml.org/packages/ifs-fractals/</a></li>
<li>Changelog: <a href=3D"https://github.com/cedricbonhomme/iterated-functi=
on-systems/blob/master/CHANGES.md">https://github.com/cedricbonhomme/iterat=
ed-function-systems/blob/master/CHANGES.md</a></li>
</ul>

<p>
Feedback and new fractal definitions are very welcome. Just make a pull-req=
uest.
</p>

<p>
If you design an attractor you like, I'd love to see it!
</p>
</div>
</div>
