<p>
Starting from the first version, two abstractions are already available to =
you, one for input (<code>Bytream.In.t</code>), and the other for outputtin=
g data (<code>Bytream.Out.t</code>). Both use <a href=3D"https://ocaml.org/=
manual/5.5/api/Bigarray.html">Bigarray</a> under the hood to represent an a=
rray of bytes. The motivation for this choice is to avoid duplication and f=
ix runtime when transferring this data to external functions.
</p>

<p>
As already mentioned, inheriting ideas from <code>Bytesrw</code>, Bytream u=
ses the mechanism of chunks to feed the stream. An example illustrates the =
